I've successfully integrated Capacitor v8 into the Vite + TypeScript project and wired up the `@capacitor/device` plugin to display device information. Here's a summary of what was done:

## Changes Made

### 1. Installed Capacitor v8 packages
- `@capacitor/core@8` (runtime dependency)
- `@capacitor/cli@8` (dev dependency)
- `@capacitor/device@8` (the Device plugin)

### 2. Initialized Capacitor (non-interactive)
Ran `npx cap init "Device Demo" "com.example.devicedemo" --web-dir dist`, which generated `capacitor.config.ts`:
```ts
import type { CapacitorConfig } from '@capacitor/cli';

const config: CapacitorConfig = {
  appId: 'com.example.devicedemo',
  appName: 'Device Demo',
  webDir: 'dist'
};

export default config;
```

### 3. Updated `index.html`
Added two elements with stable IDs whose text content reflects the live `Device.getInfo()` values:
```html
<p>Platform: <span id="device-platform">loading…</span></p>
<p>Operating System: <span id="device-os">loading…</span></p>
```
The script is loaded as an ES module (`type="module"`).

### 4. Updated `src/main.ts`
Imports `Device` from `@capacitor/device`, awaits `Device.getInfo()`, and writes the `platform` and `operatingSystem` values into `#device-platform` and `#device-os` respectively:
```ts
import { Device } from "@capacitor/device";

async function renderDeviceInfo(): Promise<void> {
  const info = await Device.getInfo();
  const platformEl = document.getElementById("device-platform");
  const osEl = document.getElementById("device-os");
  if (platformEl) platformEl.textContent = info.platform;
  if (osEl) osEl.textContent = info.operatingSystem;
}

renderDeviceInfo().catch((err) => {
  console.error("Failed to load device info:", err);
});
```

## Verification
- **`npm run build`** — succeeded, producing `dist/` with the bundled JS including the Device plugin.
- **`npx cap sync`** — ran successfully (`Sync finished in 0.012s`).
- **`npx tsc --noEmit`** — passed with no type errors.
- **`vite preview`** — confirmed the served HTML contains both `#device-platform` and `#device-os` elements.

When loaded in a browser, `Device.getInfo()` will populate `#device-platform` with `web` and `#device-os` with the detected operating system (e.g., `mac`, `windows`, `unknown`).
